    parses these yet, and baking a marker into the id format is the kind of thing you can't take back once logs exist.
  - [1m[38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID[39m unavailable.[0m It requires a secure context, so plain [38;5;153mhttp://[39m on a LAN IP doesn't have it. Fall back
    to hex from [38;5;153mcrypto.getRandomValues[39m.
  - [1mCorrupt stored value.[0m Anything that isn't a non-empty string is treated as absent and regenerated.

  [1mTesting.[0m The behavior worth pinning is exactly the above: same id across repeated calls, id survives a simulated
  reload (fresh module + populated storage), a new id when storage is empty, no throw when storage throws, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m
  returning a result whose [38;5;153muserId[39m matches [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m. All of it reachable with an injectable storage object and Node's
  built-in [38;5;153mnode --test[39m — no dependencies. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m's DOM wiring stays untested; it's a stub and an e2e harness for one
  form isn't worth it yet.

  [1mTooling.[0m This repo has no test runner, no linter, no formatter, no CI, and no [38;5;153mscripts[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. Now — before
  there's code to retrofit — is the cheapest moment to decide. Whatever you pick lands in the spec's Global Constraints
  so the implementation plan inherits it.

